    "tab:positioning"
  ],
  "caption": "Method positioning. ✓/-- indicate presence/absence of each property. Graph-based retrieval uses inter-item graph relations during retrieval. LLM-free graph initialization constructs the initial graph without LLM calls; -- also denotes methods without graph initialization. SkillGraph uses teacher-generated skill nodes even though its initial edges follow fixed rules. SE-GoS refers to the primary semantic-only initialization. Execution-driven updates use execution traces or outcomes to update system state. Training-free excludes neural-weight training of either the agent or retriever. Skill selection chooses library skills for the current task, whether or not their content is also edited. Edge-weight updates adapt numerical relation weights from feedback. Retrieval-description updates optimize descriptions for retrieval while leaving source skill content unchanged. Rows describe the original methods: MemRL retrieves episodic memories, and SkillOpt optimizes a shared skill document. Our experimental MemRL* adaptation selects skills.",
  "caption_latex": "\\textbf{Method positioning.} $\\checkmark$/-- indicate presence/absence of each property. \\emph{Graph-based retrieval} uses inter-item graph relations during retrieval. \\emph{LLM-free graph initialization} constructs the initial graph without LLM calls; -- also denotes methods without graph initialization. SkillGraph uses teacher-generated skill nodes even though its initial edges follow fixed rules. SE-GoS refers to the primary semantic-only initialization. \\emph{Execution-driven updates} use execution traces or outcomes to update system state. \\emph{Training-free} excludes neural-weight training of either the agent or retriever. \\emph{Skill selection} chooses library skills for the current task, whether or not their content is also edited. \\emph{Edge-weight updates} adapt numerical relation weights from feedback. \\emph{Retrieval-description updates} optimize descriptions for retrieval while leaving source skill content unchanged. Rows describe the original methods: MemRL retrieves episodic memories, and SkillOpt optimizes a shared skill document. Our experimental MemRL$^{*}$ adaptation selects skills.",
